      "claim": "ReadEnrollment, which used to be a pure read, now writes: it promotes the old per-install proof to the machine file, and it refreshes the install's cached copy whenever that copy differs from the machine file. If either write fails, it returns an error. Prove then refuses with 'not enrolled' before it ever consults the helm and power-of-attorney seams. So an agent the helm or a grant would admit, running where it cannot write ~/.metasystem/host or the install's cache directory, is refused. This only happens right after deploy (before the first person act promotes the file), or after the person re-enrolled from another install (which leaves this install's cache stale).",
      "evidence": "Traced path: authority.go:698-707 handles promotion-write failure, returning 'could not be promoted'. authority.go:721-725 handles cache-refresh failure, returning 'install's cache could not be refreshed'. authority.go:960-968 shows Prove returning OutcomeNotEnrolled on any ReadEnrollment error. authority.go:1036 and :1046 show the AtHelm and AtAttorney seams being reached only inside proveEnrolled, after a successful read. Not material: no reproduction. I could not show that the seat's agent sandbox denies those writes, and the delegate sandbox (adapter/claude.go) only matters for builder delegates, which are not person-acting. The design asks for promotion on first use, and the person's own terminal act repairs it. Possible later fix, if it ever causes trouble: let a failed cache refresh still return the machine enrollment instead of an error.",
